ing  however you still need to shake the  joy-con like the wii remote to do this  if you're playing with motion controls  instead of button controls and now when  you're controlling the loft wing of  button controls you only need to hit a  to gain altitude instead of shaking the  controller and the left stick controls  the loft wing instead of the pointer  also worth noting that when walking on a  tightrope with button controls you only  need to use the left stick to balance  link  and if you fall off you can click the  left stick in to jostle the rope to  knock off enemies or hazards  and on top of that the right stick is  used to swing on ropes  there are also all the items in the game  and as you might have guessed most of  these are now controlled with the right  stick and zr when playing with button  controls  the bow beetle bug net slingshot claw  shots  whip and gus bellows all work like this  and most of them have gyro controls  except for the whip and the beetle  although to clarify you can aim the  beetle with gyro but once you shoot it  you can only control it with the left  stick and no gyro  then once you unlock the harp that gets  assigned to the x button and you can  strum it with the right stick  the biggest item change though is the  bombs with motion controls you can throw  or roll the bombs with a throw or roll  motion  with the button controls you pull out a  bomb with zr then hold the right stick  up for throw or down for roll  then click zr again to do that action it  was a little hard to wrap my brain  around that at first but i got the hang  of it pretty quick  but there is one huge change that is so  significant i'm giving it its own  numbered section  number two camera controls the original  skyward sword did not have camera  controls because the wii didn't have a  second control stick  instead you can hit z on the wii  nunchuck to recenter the camera and that  functionality still exists if you hit zl  on the switch  however nintendo actually added full 360  camera controls to skyward sword hd  how did they do it well if you're  playing with motion controls all you  need to do is move the right stick  simple enough but how does it work with  the button controls  well if you hold the l button then move  the right stick you can use the camera  controls  this actually works extremely well in my  opinion and i'm very happy to see full  camera controls in skyward sword  it improved the experience a lot and i  think the right stick for the sword and  then holding l with the right stick to  move the camera is pretty clever  number three you can save to any slot in  most zelda games  including the original skyward sword  once you picked a save file at the  beginning  that became your save file every time  you saved it would go to that save file  and that was the end of that however in  skyward sword hd  nintendo changed it so every time you go  to a bird statue to save  you can choose which of the three slots  you want to save to granted  if you really wanted to you could  technically save then quit the game  then copy your file to another save slot  to preserve a specific spot in the game  but now it's much easier and more  streamlined  for example i stream skyward sword hd  here on gamespot  i knew i wanted to stream the skyview  temple so i saved over my main file then  made a separate save over a different  file that i'd used for the stream  then continued the game easy this also  helps for something else we'll talk  about a bit later  but for now you'll also notice there's a  fourth save slot  and what might that be number four  autosave  yes skyward sword hd has auto saves so  you don't have to worry about losing  save data or saving at a bird statue  every time you come across one  i'm not 100 sure just yet but after some  testing the auto saves seem to trigger  whenever you walk near a bird statue or  whenever you enter or leave a room or  area that requires a loading screen  you'll notice it in the b